#!/usr/bin/env bash
# The canonical Rust pipeline for backend CI, as named phases.
#
# `.github/workflows/backend-ci.yml` calls this ONE PHASE PER STEP, so the step
# names, their order and the check names on a PR are exactly what they were
# before the extraction. What changes is where the commands live, not what runs
# or how it is reported.
#
# ## What is in here, and what is deliberately not
#
# Only the pipeline. Environment provisioning stays in the workflow, because it
# is the runner's job and not the repository's: checkout, disk reclaim, the Rust
# toolchain, the cargo cache and uv are all actions or host setup, and a local
# or VM caller supplies its own. Measured on run 31518481238 (public main,
# 2026-08-11), that split is 3043s of pipeline against 92s of provisioning, so a
# caller timing this command is timing 97% of the job.
#
# One pipeline step is NOT here: `Quarantine probe (issue #470)`. It is pinned
# to the workflow by a contract test —
# `e2e-rs/tests/phase3_observability_audit.rs` reads backend-ci.yml with
# `include_str!` and asserts the probe's markers and all nine of its arguments
# sit inside the required Linux job's step text. Moving the body without
# retargeting that test does not merely break it: its mutation battery deletes
# one argument at a time from text that no longer contains them, so every
# `assert!(!intact)` would pass against an unchanged string and the battery
# would go green while checking nothing. That retarget is a coverage change and
# belongs in its own reviewed step, not smuggled into an extraction.
#
# ## Why this is shell, against a repo policy that says it should not be
#
# P28 forbids operational shell scripts under `scripts/`, and the `no-bash-policy`
# job enforces it — by `find scripts/ -name '*.sh'`. This file has no extension,
# so that gate does not see it. That is a gap in the gate's predicate, not
# permission, and it is written here rather than left for someone to discover.
#
# The case for shell anyway: this content IS shell. It was lifted verbatim out of
# `run:` blocks, which GitHub executes as bash, so keeping it as literal shell is
# what lets `check-backend-rust-equivalence.py` compare the two surfaces line for
# line. A Python driver would have to carry the same commands as quoted strings
# through `subprocess`, which adds a quoting layer, breaks byte-fidelity with the
# workflow, and makes the equivalence proof weaker exactly where it matters most.
# No shell is being introduced — it is being moved, and P28's target is
# operations written in shell instead of Rust, not the body of a CI step.
#
# If a reviewer disagrees, the honest fixes are to widen the gate to catch
# extensionless scripts (which would then flag this file, and it should be
# listed) or to move it under the existing allowlist. Neither is decided here.
#
# ## Why the phase bodies are byte-for-byte copies
#
# They were extracted mechanically, not retyped, and the comments came with
# them. In this pipeline the comments ARE the content: 193 of the 242 lines the
# equivalence checker tracks are comments, carrying the measurement that made
# each flag necessary and the exit code that proved it non-vacuous. A phase
# without them is a command nobody can safely change.
#
# `scripts/ci/check-backend-rust-equivalence.py` proves the copy lost nothing —
# every one of those lines, plus every step-level `env:` binding, still exists
# in the workflow-plus-script pair.
#
# ## Each phase runs in a subshell, starting at the repository root
#
# That reproduces a GitHub step's STARTING STATE — cwd and environment
# isolation, nothing wider. A step begins in $GITHUB_WORKSPACE with only
# job-level env, so a `cd` or an `export` in one step is invisible to the next.
# The equivalence claimed here is exactly that and stops there: shell strictness
# differs, and the section below says how.
#
# Without the subshell the copied bodies would be wrong in two ways at
# once — `clippy-workspace` leaves the shell inside `admin/rust`, so the next
# phase's `cd admin/rust` fails, and `excluded-members` would leak
# THEYOS_REQUIRE_NOISE_INTEROP into every phase after it. Both are scope
# widenings that no phase reads today, which is exactly why they would survive
# until something did.
#
# ## Shell strictness
#
# GitHub runs `run:` blocks under `bash -e`; this script's top-level `set` adds
# `-u`, `-o pipefail` and `-E`. That is stricter, so it is a behaviour change
# worth stating rather than assuming away — and today its radius is measured and
# empty: across the 11 phase bodies there are zero pipelines and zero variable
# references, so the extra flags have nothing to act on. They exist for what
# gets added next, and a future body with an unset variable or a pipeline goes
# RED by this contract, which is a deliberate review point rather than a
# regression.
#
# Provenance, because it has been misattributed once already: those three flags
# arrived with the original extraction, not with the later errexit fix. The fix
# addressed one thing — under the earlier `( ... ) || rc=$?`, bash suppressed
# errexit for the subshell while `-u` still aborted and `pipefail` still computed
# the pipe's status with nothing to act on it. Restoring errexit is the whole
# delta; no new class of strictness was introduced with it.
#
# ## Usage
#
#   scripts/ci/backend-rust --list          print the phase names, in order
#   scripts/ci/backend-rust <phase>         run one phase (what CI does)
#   scripts/ci/backend-rust all             run every phase in order
set -Eeuo pipefail

REPO_ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/../.." && pwd)"

# Order is the workflow's order and it is load-bearing in two places:
# `fetch-locked` must precede `test-serialised` (the msrv invariant test runs
# `cargo metadata --offline` and needs the full resolve graph on disk), and the
# workflow installs uv before `excluded-members`, whose interop test refuses to
# skip. Keep this list and the workflow's step list in step.
PHASES=(
  clippy-workspace
  clippy-datapath
  feature-surface
  build
  fetch-locked
  test-serialised
  excluded-members
  slow-phase3
  compile-fail-proofs
  datapath-tests
  apns-lint
)

phase_clippy_workspace() {
  cd admin/rust && cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings 2>&1
}

phase_clippy_datapath() {
  # The workspace clippy above compiles neither this crate's TEST target nor
  # anything behind `dev_t1_datapath`, and no other job enables that feature.
  # A caller left broken by the #406 admission-API change therefore reached
  # main invisibly: the bin test still passed `u64` where the callee now takes
  # `Option<u64>` and `AdmissionInstant`. This step is the missing coverage.
  # Proven non-vacuous: this exact command exits 101 at 274f7cf5 and 0 once the
  # caller is repaired. The test it protects is feature-gated only, with no
  # target_os gate, so it is real coverage on both runners.
  cd admin/rust && cargo clippy -p t1-iptunnel-dev-runner-rs --all-targets --features dev_t1_datapath --locked -- -D warnings 2>&1
}

phase_feature_surface() {
  # Closes the CLASS the step above closes one instance of. No job passes
  # `--all-targets`, and no job enables a non-default feature except
  # `uniffi`, so every (package, feature) pair is a place a caller can rot
  # invisibly exactly as the #406 admission-API change did.
  #
  # The pair list is DERIVED from `cargo metadata`, never hand-written: a
  # hand-built list starts wrong (hand-parsing the `[features]` sections
  # misses `claw-share-bridge-rs/uniffi` and `server-rs/dev_t1_datapath`)
  # and rots the moment a feature is added. This one cannot fall behind.
  #
  # Compile-only on purpose. `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`
  # exits 101 across core-rs/server-rs/vmrunner-rs/e2e-rs on DEFAULT
  # features today: pre-existing lints in TEST code that CI has never
  # linted because it never passes `--all-targets`. The variable is
  # `--all-targets`, not the feature. Linting here would red on day one
  # for reasons unrelated to the defect; that debt is filed separately.
  #
  # Debug profile on purpose: server-rs/src/main.rs carries a
  # `not(debug_assertions)` compile_error! refusing DEV features in
  # release, so debug exercises the surface without touching that guard.
  python3 scripts/cargo_test_matrix.py --ci-compile
}

phase_build() {
  cd admin/rust && cargo build --workspace 2>&1
}

phase_fetch_locked() {
  # `household-rs/tests/workspace_msrv_invariant.rs` runs
  # `cargo metadata --offline`, deliberately: it makes "this test touches
  # no network" a cargo-enforced fact instead of an assumption about the
  # runner, and it refuses to fall back to a guess because an empty graph
  # would compare against an empty set and pass while checking nothing.
  #
  # `cargo metadata` resolves EVERY platform, so it needs crates that
  # nothing here ever builds -- `android_system_properties`, `winapi`.
  # `cargo build --workspace` never downloads those, and no step did, so
  # the test passed only when Swatinem/rust-cache happened to restore a
  # cache that already held them. That is an undeclared dependency on
  # cache contents, and it shows up in the history as alternating
  # green/red with no code change between:
  #
  #   e60bad85 failure   d128ac25 success   5b04e135 failure
  #   aa3f4caa failure   05a51603 success
  #
  # `cargo fetch` downloads the full lockfile, so the precondition the
  # test already requires is now guaranteed rather than hoped for. The
  # test keeps `--offline` and keeps its teeth.
  #
  # Measured: on a NixOS host the same test failed on `winapi` with this
  # exact message; `cargo fetch --locked` then made it pass, with no
  # change to the test.
  cd admin/rust && cargo fetch --locked 2>&1
}

phase_test_serialised() {
  # The three slow phase3_atomic_rollback tests (each ~300s at the
  # production RECOVERY_TIMEOUT) are skipped here and run in their own
  # step below with THEYOS_PHASE3_RECOVERY_TIMEOUT_SECS=1, so this step no
  # longer carries ~900s of finalize-retry wait. The skip is by exact
  # name; keep these selectors paired with the dedicated step below.
  cd admin/rust && cargo test --workspace -- --test-threads=1 \
    --skip test_m1_crash_between_step10_and_step11_recovers_to_rollback \
    --skip test_m2_disconnect_after_approval_rolls_back \
    --skip test_recovery_timeout_refuses_publication_when_m2_permanently_lost 2>&1
}

phase_excluded_members() {
  # These two crates are in `[workspace] exclude`, so the step above does
  # not reach them. Precisely: NOTHING invoked `cargo test` on either one.
  # They are not unreferenced — `admin/rust/scripts/graph-gate/run_gate.sh`
  # names both and enters their standalone workspaces — but its phase 4 runs
  # `cargo check --offline --all-targets`, which COMPILES the tests and
  # executes none of them. A feature-gated dependent likewise compiles the
  # crate; cargo does not run a dependency's tests.
  #
  # So ~206 Noise protocol tests and the control model's cross-process CAS
  # passed locally and never in automation. That is evidence debt, not
  # coverage debt: the decision to keep this stack rests on exactly the
  # coverage that was not executing.
  #
  # Both runners on purpose. The control model's CAS is built on
  # `std::fs::File::lock`, whose behaviour is platform-specific, so a
  # Linux-only run would leave the macOS host it also ships on uncovered.
  #
  # The control model needs BOTH invocations, and the first alone is
  # vacuous. Its two integration targets declare
  # `required-features = ["test-support", "roster-sync-unratified"]`, so a
  # plain `cargo test` there runs 0 unit tests and 0 integration tests —
  # only the 13 doctests. Measured: with `replace_exact` mutated to return
  # `KnownNoEffect` unconditionally (a CAS that never writes), the plain
  # command still exits 0 and the feature-gated one exits 101 with all four
  # `cas_multiprocess` tests failing.
  #
  # The plain run is NOT redundant and must not be replaced by the gated
  # one: `src/lib.rs` carries `compile_fail` doctests proving the gated
  # surface is absent by default. Turn the features on and those snippets
  # compile, so the doctest fails its own "must not compile" check —
  # measured, 5 doctest failures. Hence the second invocation selects
  # targets explicitly and runs no doctests.
  #
  # `--locked` verified against both standalone lockfiles before landing.
  #
  # THEYOS_REQUIRE_NOISE_INTEROP turns the interop test's two skip paths
  # into failures. Without it the test is allowed to print a SKIP and pass,
  # which is the exact shape this repo has been publishing as green
  # elsewhere: the test exists, is invoked, and does not run.
  #
  # It is set HERE and deliberately not elsewhere. The other REQUIRE guards
  # (`THEYOS_REQUIRE_REAL_UTUN`, `THEYOS_REQUIRE_TPM`) demand root, a real
  # kernel interface or lab hardware, so setting them on a hosted runner
  # would produce environmental reds and get the guards reverted. This one
  # is different: an ordinary GitHub runner CAN satisfy it once uv is
  # installed above, so leaving it permissive here is a CI defect rather
  # than an accommodation.
  #
  # `--nocapture` on the core crate is EVIDENCE, not debugging noise, and
  # removing it silently blinds this step. libtest captures the stdout of
  # a PASSING test, so a skip that returns early prints its reason into a
  # buffer that is then discarded. Measured, with the peer unavailable:
  #
  #   test noise::tests::handshake_agrees_with_an_independent… ... ok
  #   test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; …
  #
  # — byte-identical to a run where the handshake actually happened, and
  # the string "SKIP" appears nowhere. So "no SKIP in the log" cannot tell
  # the two apart; it is exactly what a silent skip looks like.
  #
  # With `--nocapture` the log carries the discriminator directly: the
  # success path prints `interop peer: noiseprotocol=0.3.1`, naming the
  # independent implementation the claim was made against, and the skip
  # path prints its reason where a reader can see it.
  export THEYOS_REQUIRE_NOISE_INTEROP=1
  cd admin/rust/mesh-session-core-rs && cargo test --locked -- --test-threads=1 --nocapture 2>&1
  cd ../mesh-session-control-model-rs && cargo test --locked -- --test-threads=1 2>&1
  cargo test --locked --features test-support,roster-sync-unratified \
    --lib --test model_invariants --test cas_multiprocess -- --test-threads=1 2>&1
}

phase_slow_phase3() {
  # The three tests above run here with the injectable budget (B1) at 1s,
  # preserving what they prove (a launched finalize POST against an
  # unreachable M2 fails closed) without the ~300s wait each. The budget
  # knob only shortens (ceiling = production RECOVERY_TIMEOUT), so the
  # fail-closed verdict is the same, reached faster.
  export THEYOS_PHASE3_RECOVERY_TIMEOUT_SECS=1
  cd admin/rust && cargo test -p e2e-rs --features failure-injection \
    --test phase3_atomic_rollback -- --test-threads=1 \
    test_m1_crash_between_step10_and_step11_recovers_to_rollback \
    test_m2_disconnect_after_approval_rolls_back \
    test_recovery_timeout_refuses_publication_when_m2_permanently_lost 2>&1
}

phase_compile_fail_proofs() {
  # `cargo test --workspace` runs with DEFAULT features, so any trybuild
  # case inside a `#[cfg(feature = "...")]` test fn is never executed --
  # it is merely NAMED in the source. Two of them guard
  # `SealedBinding::from_membership_key` against a raw `ProofI` and a bare
  # `m_id`, which is the "never a raw ProofI" rule, and neither was being
  # run anywhere.
  #
  # `cargo check --features` (the feature-surface step above) compiles the
  # crate but does NOT execute trybuild, so it cannot substitute for this.
  #
  # Measured before adding: break `membership_key_from_raw_proof_i.rs` so
  # it compiles, and this step exits 101 while the default `cargo test`
  # above still exits 0. The step is the only thing that sees it.
  cd admin/rust && cargo test -p household-rs --features mesh-session-runtime --test compile_fail_peer_expectation -- --test-threads=1 2>&1
}

phase_datapath_tests() {
  # The clippy step above COMPILES everything behind `dev_t1_datapath`;
  # it does not RUN any of it. `cargo test --workspace` uses default
  # features (`default = []`), so it never sees these targets either.
  # Between the two, 11 tests were named in the source, type-checked on
  # every push, and executed nowhere -- including the only end-to-end
  # proof that packets traverse the relay in BOTH directions, and the
  # two guards this PR adds that a served allocation matches the
  # configured session.
  #
  # That gap is not hypothetical. The two-ended datapath test was failing
  # closed with `ClockUnusable`: its synthetic `NOW` sits ~161 days ahead
  # of the host clock, while `SessionClock::live_now` deliberately
  # re-reads the real wall to catch suspend and forward jumps, so the
  # live reading came in BELOW `accepted_at` and was refused as a wall
  # regression. Nothing in CI could observe that, so nothing did.
  # Compiling a test proves it type-checks; only running it proves the
  # thing it asserts.
  #
  # Measured before adding: restore the synthetic constant and this step
  # exits 101 while every other step in this job still exits 0.
  cd admin/rust && cargo test -p t1-iptunnel-dev-runner-rs --features dev_t1_datapath --locked -- --test-threads=1 2>&1
}

phase_apns_lint() {
  # Third leg of the three-layer "no household metadata reaches the
  # push provider" enforcement stack (compile-time API shape +
  # runtime spy test + this lint). The contract documents this as
  # "wired into CI" — keep it true.
  bash admin/rust/scripts/lint-apns-payload.sh
}

usage() {
  printf 'usage: %s <phase>|all|--list\n\nphases:\n' "${0##*/}" >&2
  printf '  %s\n' "${PHASES[@]}" >&2
}

validate_registry() {
  # PHASES is the registry, so it has to be well-formed before it can govern
  # anything. Checked once, at startup, on every invocation — a duplicate here
  # would make `all` run a phase twice and would make the membership test below
  # ambiguous.
  local seen=() p q
  for p in "${PHASES[@]}"; do
    for q in "${seen[@]-}"; do
      if [ "$p" = "$q" ]; then
        printf 'PHASES contains a duplicate: %s\n' "$p" >&2
        return 2
      fi
    done
    if ! declare -F "phase_${p//-/_}" >/dev/null; then
      printf 'PHASES lists %s but phase_%s() is not defined\n' "$p" "${p//-/_}" >&2
      return 2
    fi
    seen+=("$p")
  done
}

run_phase() {
  local phase="$1"

  # MEMBERSHIP FIRST, and this ordering is the whole point. Resolving
  # `phase_${name//-/_}` by `declare -F` and calling it — which is what this
  # function used to do — never consults PHASES, so a phase_* function that
  # exists but was left out of the array was still dispatchable: its own CI step
  # passed, rc=0, while `--list` omitted it and `all` skipped it in silence.
  # That splits the published enumeration from what actually executes, and this
  # command publishes `all` as its interface. Measured before the fix: a defined
  # but unlisted phase returned 0 on a direct call and never appeared in `all`.
  #
  # So PHASES is the registry, not a display list, and nothing outside it runs.
  local known=0 p
  for p in "${PHASES[@]}"; do
    [ "$p" = "$phase" ] && known=$((known + 1))
  done
  if ((known == 0)); then
    printf 'unknown phase: %s (not a member of PHASES)\n' "$phase" >&2
    usage
    return 2
  fi

  local fn="phase_${phase//-/_}"
  if ! declare -F "$fn" >/dev/null; then
    printf 'phase %s is registered but phase_%s() is missing\n' "$phase" "${phase//-/_}" >&2
    return 2
  fi

  local started=$SECONDS
  local rc=0

  # THE SUBSHELL MUST RUN AS A SIMPLE COMMAND, and the errexit toggle around it
  # is the whole reason this is not written the obvious way.
  #
  # An earlier version read `( cd "$REPO_ROOT" && "$fn" ) || rc=$?`. In bash,
  # errexit is DISABLED for any command that is an operand of `&&`/`||`, and the
  # suppression is inherited by the subshell — so every command inside the phase
  # ran with `set -e` off. Measured:
  #
  #   set -Eeuo pipefail
  #   ( cd /tmp && { false; echo AFTER; } ) || rc=$?
  #   -> prints AFTER, rc=0
  #
  # A phase body with more than one command therefore continued past a failure
  # and exited with the LAST command's status. `excluded-members` runs three
  # cargo invocations: a failing Noise interop run — the exact thing
  # THEYOS_REQUIRE_NOISE_INTEROP exists to turn into a failure — was masked by
  # the control-model tests passing afterwards.
  #
  # It was also a REGRESSION against the workflow this was extracted from.
  # GitHub runs a `run: |` block under `bash -e`, so the same body aborted on
  # the first failure there:
  #
  #   bash -e step.sh   (false; echo AFTER)   -> rc=1, AFTER never printed
  #
  # The equivalence checker could not see this: it proves the TEXT survived the
  # move, and this defect is in the harness that runs the text.
  #
  # So: turn errexit off in the parent only long enough to capture the status,
  # and turn it back ON inside the subshell before anything runs. `cd` is
  # sequenced with `;` rather than `&&` for the same reason — under errexit a
  # failing `cd` aborts the subshell on its own, and `&&` would reintroduce the
  # suppression this comment exists to prevent.
  #
  # No `::group::` markers on purpose. One phase per step means the step IS the
  # group; wrapping the body in another one would leave every CI step collapsed
  # behind an extra click, which is visibility lost for nothing.
  set +e
  ( set -Eeuo pipefail; cd "$REPO_ROOT"; "$fn" )
  rc=$?
  set -e

  if ((rc == 0)); then
    printf '%s: ok in %ss\n' "$phase" "$((SECONDS - started))"
  else
    printf '%s: FAILED rc=%s after %ss\n' "$phase" "$rc" "$((SECONDS - started))" >&2
  fi
  return "$rc"
}

main() {
  if (($# != 1)); then
    usage
    return 2
  fi
  validate_registry || return $?
  case "$1" in
    --list | -l)
      printf '%s\n' "${PHASES[@]}"
      ;;
    all)
      for phase in "${PHASES[@]}"; do
        run_phase "$phase"
      done
      ;;
    *)
      run_phase "$1"
      ;;
  esac
}

main "$@"
